thank you and thank you for inviting me  to be on this panel which caused me to  stop and reflect and to really come to  appreciate in a way I hadn't before how  much I owe the Yale graduate school I  want to dedicate my remarks to two  people kingman brewster for whom I  served as chief of staff and my  dissertation advisor John Morton Blum  who died a couple weeks ago and we had  his service here in battelle just just a  week ago tomorrow what I'm about to say  reflects what I learned from them both  in and beyond the classroom I majored in  history here as an undergraduate so I  was doubly blessed studied twice with  the likes of ed Morgan Howard Lamar then  Woodward gatos meath John Blum and what  was I think still the Golden Age of  American history at Yale and since I've  been privileged to lead the new school  as it reconnected to its European roots  by helping dissident scholars and East  and Central Europe in the 1980s Linda  chair Human Rights Watch as it expanded  to Africa and Asia to be president of  the MacArthur Foundation which works on  community development and education in  the US and globally on conservation  population Human Rights peace and  security in 60 countries and happy to  say with an office in Mexico and I have  no doubt that my apprenticeship with  Kingman and my historical training at  the Graduate School were key ingredients  to whatever good I've been able to do in  any of those leadership opportunities so  let me cite seven lessons and learnings  the first is that leadership can be  dominated by problems and can be  reactive but history tells me and  example reaffirms that moments of crisis  can be turned to good purpose and so  I've always looked for the opportunity  in a problem not been put on the  defensive and consumed by the negative  and put my energy in to as the song says  accentuating the positive number two  John Blum used to say and I can hear it  now nothing is inevitable people can  make or shape the course and turn the  tide of history and that advice was  never more useful than in Prague 1989  when I just by accident happened to be  sitting at the key moment with the  leaders of the Velvet Revolution when  they were trying to decide whether this  was the moment whether to press ahead  and pursue this revolution if they had  it wrong they'd be in jail and if they  didn't press ahead and it was the moment  they would forever be regretful third my  approach to any situation whether unrest  on campus and I had lots of that to deal  with here Yale ethnic strife in Africa  or struggles to conserve land rich in  biodiversity my approach is to  understand the historical context and  how different issues intersect and as  gana Smith taught me try to appreciate  the life experience of the people in  conflict or who must make common cause  to achieve a desired outcome so my first  step in any situation is an historical  analysis trying to understand who's  there what their history is how they see  the what lens they see the problem  through number four is timing matters  and I'll never forget stopping a walk  with King went across the quadrant angle  there by the fly  and had a really tough time during May  Day and he said you know when you run an  institution you have to know when to be  decisive or peer decisive on occasion to  be indecisive or appear indecisive and  so we all know there are opportunities  missed because the moment passed didn't  act when we should have wore failure was  suffered because the moment for action  wasn't right so knowing where you are in  the arc of history has been important to  me in deciding where to invest time and  effort and that's never been more  critical than my human rights work where  are the problems around the world are so  vast the resources to confront those  problems so inadequate and so you have  to pick your moments you have to know  get the timing right number five and  here I think of Howard llamar history  helps us understand the problems are not  solved or opportunity seized in the  short run patience is important as is  the capacity to peer over the horizon  and project forward from historical  trends and progress is ordinarily made  in increments provided there are there  are purposeful steps that are understood  and placed in a context in historical  context so progress is really a nut  proposition rarely a study linear march  but hope determination and motivation to  sacrifice and risk depend on some  visible reinforcements and so when I was  at MacArthur I understood if you wanted  to advance human rights in Russia  probably not going to confront the  Kremlin have on but you could take on an  issue like police reform the aspect of  government ordinary people see every day  the  lease and try to get that right or in  China very hard to work but we were able  to start a system of public defenders  working in the rural countryside or in  Cuba not an easy place to work but there  an authoritarian government persuaded  that conservation was it's an best  interest moved ahead in a very good way  number six getting close to the end I  think it was Vann Woodward who helped me  understand how to seek common ground how  to search for compromise without  sacrificing core principles and also to  be careful about strident ideology  invoked with too much passion that  narrows a space for reasonable people to  differ but to go on persevere and search  and finally from ed Morgan I've always  believed that ideas matter you can be  cynical but I'm not I do believe that  ideas influence policy values and  principles grounded in rigorous thinking  and expressed in charter documents can  make a difference over time and the  international human rights movement  fortified by treaties and covenants most  noticeably the universal declaration is  a good example of how ideas can  gradually change behavior and that's  happened really since 78 most  dramatically so in conclusion I never  had a second thought about pursuing  history rather than law which had been  my alternative but when I came to the  graduate school in my knight and my 30s  I understood I was never going to be a  great scholar even though I love  teaching but I believe that a deep  immersion in history was the best  preparation I could have for a life of  public service and so i'm grateful that  i did my study here at a university and  a graduate school which honors and  encourages giving back to society  thank you
 

